1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,664
Through which machine does an online payment go?

2
00:00:02,714 --> 00:00:05,400
In a shop, you pay at a card machine on the counter.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:11,616
Online there is no counter, but the processing centre still wants to know which machine sent each charge.

4
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:15,192
It accepts charges only from machines it has issued.

5
00:00:15,242 --> 00:00:21,576
Send a charge with the wrong numbers, and the money is refused, or worse, booked to another merchant.

6
00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:24,672
A taxi cannot take fares without a licence plate.

7
00:00:24,722 --> 00:00:30,816
A plate is issued once, one per car, and never reused, even after the car is scrapped.

8
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:32,688
Here is how it works.

9
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:37,776
Each online payment goes through a virtual card machine: an e-POS terminal.

10
00:00:37,826 --> 00:00:43,704
Its plate is a pair of numbers that the processing centre issues: a merchant number and a terminal number.

11
00:00:43,754 --> 00:00:46,848
On screen they are called the MID and the TID.

12
00:00:46,898 --> 00:00:51,048
A bank admin registers the pair for one website and one card.

13
00:00:51,098 --> 00:00:54,480
There is only one live terminal per website and card.

14
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:59,136
And a pair is never registered twice, not even after its terminal is closed.

15
00:00:59,186 --> 00:01:03,672
Where a processing centre requires it, the terminal is registered with the centre too.

16
00:01:03,722 --> 00:01:06,000
It is kept only if the centre accepts it.

17
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:10,440
If the centre refuses, or does not answer, nothing is saved.

18
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:12,384
An admin can suspend a terminal.

19
00:01:12,434 --> 00:01:16,176
That stops its payments on the bank's side alone, and it can be resumed.

20
00:01:16,226 --> 00:01:18,048
Revoking is final.

21
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:21,744
Every change is written down: who, what, and why.

22
00:01:21,794 --> 00:01:27,288
And before every charge, the payment asks for the live terminal of its website and card.

23
00:01:27,338 --> 00:01:30,936
No terminal, no charge, and no money moves.

24
00:01:30,986 --> 00:01:32,808
Now watch the product.

25
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:35,808
The viewer is a bank admin on the website's page.

26
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:44,544
There is a section called e-POS terminals, with a hint: each card needs one live terminal from the processing centre, or its payments are refused.

27
00:01:44,594 --> 00:01:50,184
On a website with no cards chosen yet, the "Register e-POS terminal" button is grey.

28
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:54,144
Here the website already takes one card, so the button is live.

29
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:56,016
The viewer clicks it.

30
00:01:56,066 --> 00:01:59,952
A form asks for the MID, the TID and the card.

31
00:02:00,002 --> 00:02:02,976
They type the pair the centre issued and choose the card.

32
00:02:03,026 --> 00:02:05,568
The page says the terminal is registered.

33
00:02:05,618 --> 00:02:09,216
It appears in the list as Live, with who registered it.

34
00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:13,728
Next to it are three buttons: Suspend, Resume and Revoke.

35
00:02:13,778 --> 00:02:16,368
The viewer opens Revoke, just to read it.

36
00:02:16,418 --> 00:02:21,912
The window warns that a revoked pair never comes back, and asks why, for the record.

37
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:23,832
They close it without revoking.

38
00:02:23,882 --> 00:02:26,928
Now the card at the top, "Before it takes payments".

39
00:02:26,978 --> 00:02:31,728
The terminal step is ticked: every card has a live e-POS terminal.

40
00:02:31,778 --> 00:02:35,160
With the fee from the last lesson, every step is done.

41
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:39,168
The card turns green and says "Ready: taking payments".

42
00:02:39,218 --> 00:02:41,040
What does this protect?

43
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:45,072
A charge with the wrong pair can lose money, or book it to someone else.

44
00:02:45,122 --> 00:02:49,368
A pair used twice mixes two merchants' histories forever.

45
00:02:49,418 --> 00:02:54,576
One live terminal per website and card means each payment has exactly one way out.

46
00:02:54,626 --> 00:03:01,056
Asking for the live terminal before every charge means a suspended or missing terminal stops money before it moves.

47
00:03:01,106 --> 00:03:06,720
This protects the merchant's money, the buyer's charge, and the bank's books with the processing centre.

48
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:12,816
So, like a taxi's licence plate: one per car, never reused, and no plate, no fares.

49
00:03:12,866 --> 00:03:17,040
Next: how the bank knows a request really comes from the merchant.
